1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,326
Part 3 Annie: OK, Jack.

2
00:00:02,326 --> 00:00:06,040
Before we plan our presentation about refrigeration

3
00:00:06,060 --> 00:00:08,840
let's discuss what we've discovered so far.

4
00:00:08,840 --> 00:00:12,500
Jack: Fine, Annie. Though I have to admit I haven't done much research yet.

5
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:17,200
Annie: Nor me. But I found an interesting article about icehouses.

6
00:00:17,200 --> 00:00:21,267
I'd seen some 18th- and 19th-century ones here in the UK,

7
00:00:21,267 --> 00:00:26,260
so I knew they were often built in a shady area or underground

8
00:00:26,640 --> 00:00:29,400
close to lakes that might freeze in the winter.

9
00:00:29,400 --> 00:00:34,064
Then blocks of ice could be cut and stored in the icehouse.

10
00:00:34,064 --> 00:00:38,700
But I didn't realise that insulating the blocks with straw or sawdust

11
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:41,060
meant they didn't melt for months.

12
00:00:41,060 --> 00:00:43,890
The ancient Romans had refrigeration, too.

13
00:00:43,890 --> 00:00:44,890
Jack: I didn't know that.

14
00:00:44,890 --> 00:00:47,829
Annie: Yes, pits were dug in the ground,

15
00:00:47,829 --> 00:00:50,280
and snow was imported from the mountains

16
00:00:50,780 --> 00:00:53,340
even though they were at quite a distance.

17
00:00:53,340 --> 00:00:58,200
The snow was stored in the pits. Ice formed at the bottom of it

18
00:00:58,440 --> 00:01:01,050
Both the ice and the snow were then sold.

19
00:01:01,050 --> 00:01:03,320
The ice cost more than the snow

20
00:01:03,580 --> 00:01:08,070
and my guess is that only the wealthy members of society could afford it.

21
00:01:08,070 --> 00:01:13,780
Jack: I wouldn't be surprised. I also came across an article about modern domestic fridges.

22
00:01:13,780 --> 00:01:16,140
Several different technologies are used

23
00:01:16,340 --> 00:01:18,620
but they were too complex for me to understand.

24
00:01:18,620 --> 00:01:22,220
Annie: You have to wonder what happens when people get rid of old ones.

25
00:01:22,220 --> 00:01:25,260
Jack: You mean because the gases in them are harmful for the environment?

26
00:01:25,260 --> 00:01:31,938
Annie: Exactly. At least there are now plenty of organisations that will recycle most of the components safely,

27
00:01:31,938 --> 00:01:36,390
but of course some people just dump old fridges in the countryside.

28
00:01:36,390 --> 00:01:39,752
Jack: It's hard to see how they can be stopped unfortunately.

29
00:01:39,752 --> 00:01:43,420
In the UK we get rid of three million a year altogether!

30
00:01:43,420 --> 00:01:48,040
Annie: That sounds a lot, especially because fridges hardly ever break down.

31
00:01:48,040 --> 00:01:52,644
Jack: That's right. In this country we keep domestic fridges for 11 years on average,

32
00:01:52,644 --> 00:01:55,100
and a lot last for 20 or more.

33
00:01:55,100 --> 00:01:59,607
So if you divide the cost by the number of years you can use a fridge,

34
00:01:59,607 --> 00:02:01,860
they're not expensive, compared with some household appliances.

35
00:02:01,860 --> 00:02:09,330
Annie: True. I suppose manufacturers encourage people to spend more by making them different colours and designs.

36
00:02:09,330 --> 00:02:14,280
I'm sure when my parents bought their first fridge they had hardly any choice!

37
00:02:14,280 --> 00:02:16,110
Jack: Yes, there's been quite a change.

38
00:02:16,110 --> 00:02:19,597
Jack: Right, let's make a list of topics to cover in our presentation,

39
00:02:19,597 --> 00:02:22,280
and decide who's going to do more research on them.

40
00:02:22,280 --> 00:02:25,160
Then later, we can get together and plan the next step.

41
00:02:25,160 --> 00:02:29,771
Annie: OK. How about starting with how useful refrigeration is,

42
00:02:29,771 --> 00:02:33,920
and the range of goods that are refrigerated nowadays?

43
00:02:33,920 --> 00:02:37,000
Because of course it's not just food and drinks.

44
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:40,790
Jack: No, I suppose flowers and medicines are refrigerated, too.

45
00:02:40,790 --> 00:02:45,740
Annie: And computers. I could do that, unless you particularly want to.

46
00:02:45,740 --> 00:02:47,800
Jack: No, that's fine by me

47
00:02:48,100 --> 00:02:50,870
What about the effects of refrigeration on people's health?

48
00:02:50,870 --> 00:02:55,379
After all, some of the chemicals used in the 19th century were pretty harmful,

49
00:02:55,379 --> 00:03:00,210
but there have been lots of benefits too, like always having access to fresh food.

50
00:03:00,210 --> 00:03:01,780
Do you fancy dealing with that?

51
00:03:01,780 --> 00:03:04,430
Annie: I'm not terribly keen, to be honest.

52
00:03:04,430 --> 00:03:09,200
Jack: Nor me. My mind just goes blank when I read anything about chemicals.

53
00:03:09,200 --> 00:03:14,500
Annie: Oh, all right then, I'll do you a favour. But you owe me, Jack.

54
00:03:14,500 --> 00:03:18,208
OK. What about the effects on food producers,

55
00:03:18,208 --> 00:03:26,550
like farmers in poorer countries being able to export their produce to developed countries? Something for you, maybe?

56
00:03:26,550 --> 00:03:29,240
Jack: I don't mind. It should be quite interesting.

57
00:03:29,240 --> 00:03:34,107
Annie: I think we should also look at how refrigeration has helped whole cities -

58
00:03:34,107 --> 00:03:37,660
like Las Vegas, which couldn't exist without refrigeration

59
00:03:37,660 --> 00:03:39,670
because it's in the middle of a desert.

60
00:03:39,670 --> 00:03:45,540
Jack: Right. I had a quick look at an economics book in the library that's got a chapter about this sort of thing.

61
00:03:45,540 --> 00:03:48,200
I could give you the title, if you want to do this section.

62
00:03:48,200 --> 00:03:50,380
Annie: Not particularly, to be honest

63
00:03:50,840 --> 00:03:53,910
I find economics books pretty heavy going, as a rule.

64
00:03:53,910 --> 00:03:55,450
Jack: OK, leave it to me, then.

65
00:03:55,450 --> 00:04:01,127
Annie: Thanks. Then there's transport, and the difference that refrigerated trucks have made.

66
00:04:01,127 --> 00:04:04,620
I wouldn't mind having a go at that.

67
00:04:04,620 --> 00:04:08,239
Jack: Don't forget trains, too. I read something about milk

68
00:04:08,239 --> 00:04:12,080
and butter being transported in refrigerated railroad cars in the USA

69
00:04:12,080 --> 00:04:14,030
right back in the 1840s.

70
00:04:14,030 --> 00:04:16,800
Annie: I hadn't thought of trains. Thanks.

71
00:04:16,800 --> 00:04:19,665
Jack: Shall we have a separate section on domestic fridges?

72
00:04:19,665 --> 00:04:21,670
After all, they're something everyone's familiar with.

73
00:04:21,670 --> 00:04:24,701
Annie: What about splitting it into two?

74
00:04:24,701 --> 00:04:28,320
You could investigate 19th- and 20th- century fridges

75
00:04:28,320 --> 00:04:31,628
and I'll concentrate on what's available these days,

76
00:04:31,628 --> 00:04:36,390
and how manufacturers differentiate their products from those of their competitors.

77
00:04:36,390 --> 00:04:37,000
Jack: OK, that'd suit me.
